    the full feature. Costs: the options would be invented by me rather than driven by a need, and a theme toggle on a
    page with no styling is busywork. This is the YAGNI risk.

  [1mMy recommendation: the module plus one real setting.[0m It's the smallest version that makes the headline claim actually
  true, and designing the interface against one real caller is what keeps it from being wrong. On "remember username"
  specifically — a username is not a credential and storing it is standard, but it is mildly identifying on a shared
  machine, so it'd be opt-in via an unchecked-by-default box, with the password never touched.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How far should this task go? → Module + one setting (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last set of questions before I present the design. This project has no tooling configured at all — no linter, no test
  runner, no dependencies — and cheapest time to decide that is now, before code exists.

  Relevant constraint: the module targets the browser ([38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m), while [38;5;153msrc/[39m is CommonJS Node. Testing it means
  either Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m with a small injected storage fake (zero dependencies, matches the repo's current
  dependency-free state, and requires designing the module to take its storage as an injectable rather than reaching for
  the global), or a real DOM environment via vitest/jsdom (heavier, but tests the browser globals as they actually
  are). I lean zero-dependency — injecting the storage is better design regardless, and it makes the module testable
  without a DOM.

